## Chapter 1.7: Disappearance

The garbled text came as a surprise for Luka. In all of his previous visions, everything seemed so clear. Yet, thinking about it, his visions weren’t exactly crystal clear. There were some blurry regions that he thought it was just a mental haze or some practical effects his mind added in to make it looks cool and mysterious.

But why would this vision completely undecipherable? The only conclusion he reached was that the future was not yet determined but… why? 

“So… how was it? Saw anything?” Nadya asked loudly, pulling him back to the real world.

Luka let his vision fade and put the thought behind for now, “…Unfortunately, I saw a completely garbled text. I couldn’t make out any thing,” he shook his head lightly to get rid vision’s remnants.

“Huh… Maybe this paper is no good? Let me try something else…” Nadya searched her bag to see if there was other useful items.

Luka continued his thought process again. Why would the future not yet determined? Perhaps, he got unlucky with his vision and what he saw was before the case was solved? But why would it be undecipherable? It was almost like there was some kind of overlap…

Something clicked in his head. He didn’t claim that he got it all figure out but this was probably the best theoretical model he managed to come up with.

“Alright… How about this one?” Nadya was about to hand him a scrap of paper, probably asking to test the same thing but with traditional paper instead.

“No. I don’t think this will work either. And I think I might be able to explain why,” Luka pushed the paper back to her.

Nadya and Lin adjusted them to hear Luka’s explanation.

Luka glanced to the side, recheck ing his theory in his head one more time before started, “So, my vision works like this. Let’s use what we are trying to do as an example.”

“We are trying to get the information from the future. We initially assumed that my vision can see into the _linear_ future. One straight path.” 

“But it turned out that the future branches out into multiple possibilities. My vision takes from the past to the present as a reference point and look forward. Our case is still fresh, so the there are multiple branches, and they overlap, making my vision garbled.”

Nadya and Lin nodded along, they started to understand where his theory is going.

“But let’s say, instead of promising that you will write down the case summary, you write down our receipt id instead. My vision would be clear and exact because the future converges together or at least, doesn’t diverges as much.”

He finished his explanation but he did leave a room for proof which Nadya immediately follow up.

“That makes sense but I figure we should test it out,” Nadya pulled out her e-paper again.

“I will write down our receipt id on this paper,” she swore to the universe.

Luka activated his vision again and it seemed that his theory was been validated. There are some blurriness since it was written by hand, so the position overlapped lightly but there were enough possibilities that Nadya wrote approximate at the same place to make the text legible. He also noticed two extra lines of text on the page.

“Alright, got it, 211804140266 for the id. And you also write ‘Hello, Luka from the past’ below that too,” Luka chuckled lightly at future Nadya playfulness.

Lin also chuckled while Nadya tilted her head admittedly. 

“Hey, Nadya, can I write something on that?” Lin asked.

“Yeah, sure.” Nadya handed her the paper and stylus.

Lin quickly hastily wrote something in Chinese at the corner of the page. Once Lin was about to finish writing it, Luka whispered to her with a mischievous grin, “And yes, Sis, you wrote ‘If you see this, send me Nadya’s contact later’”

Lin face turned red as she clicked her tongue and gave Luka a teasing punch on the arm. Nadya looked at them curiously, oblivious to Lin attraction to her.

As Lin handed it back, Nadya asked, “What did you write here in the corner?”

Lin sneakily lied, “The same as you, but in Chinese,” she leaned into take a sip from the her glass.

Nadya didn’t doubt it one bit and casually said to Luka, “Well, I should probably frame this in my house, so your vision got a clear view of this, huh?” 

Lin almost spitted out her drink. The thought of her sneaky contact request being framed on the wall was extremely embarrassing but there was nothing she can do about it. 

After she composed herself and wiped her mouth with a paper towel, Lin steered the conversation back to Luka, “so we know that we can’t just cheat and get the answer, what should we do next?”

“Well, naturally we should pay Anatoley a visit, I can look up his address with my pocket terminal,” Nadya replied.

“Should you be using the terminal off-duty? Will that get you in trouble?” Luka asked out of concern.

“It’s fine. Just a couple of queries wouldn’t get me flagged. Though, I can only do basic research here. I can only request full biography from an archivist at the station.”

After a moment, Nadya looked up Anatoley using the pocket terminal. What she got was the surface-level public information: name, gender, age, occupation, mobile number, email, address. 
What caught Nadya eyes was that his home was on the south side of the town. The crime scene is up north, what would be the reason for him to be at the scrapyard so late at night?

Even they knew that Anatoley was dead, a weak lead is still a lead. They decided to head out together to Anatoley’s residence.

After the checkout at the cafe, Nadya checked her receipt and unsurprisingly, the id matched what Luka had said, solidifying her trust in his ability. She then wrote the down her greeting to the past Luka, rolled the paper and put it in her bag.

15 minutes later they got off the bus to the south-side residential district. This part of town is always peaceful due to low traffic and activity in general. Anatoley’s house is a block away from the bus stop.

They arrived before the front yard of the house. The snow covered most of the ground including the walkway, Anatoley didn’t seem to a neatly person.

Before anyone took a step inside the yard, Lin pointed out,

“Hey! There! Footprints!” 

A single pair of footprint leading away from the door and ended at the sidewalk where it was overlapped with other footprints.

Lin raised her camera and took a quick snap of the intact footprints before they trample it get to the doorbell.

Nadya who had the most legal authority volunteered to ring the doorbell. To no one surprise, there was no answer. They checked the door and it locked also.

“So it seems that he really is dead. Rest in peace, Comrade Ruzhin…” Nadya let out a sigh.

“At least we know now that he headed out somewhere yesterday,” Lin said, gesturing at the footprint.

Luka looked back at their footprints and notice a certain difference, “Ruzhin’s footprint looks shallow and faded compare to ours. When did it snow yesterday?”

“If I’m not wrong, it started around 8 PM, but I’m not sure when did it end,” Lin replied.

“In that case, we can be sure that he headed out before 8 PM. The only question is where? Did he head straight to the scrapyard right away?” Nadya pondered.

While the three of them pondering about Anatoley’s journey yesterday, a voice called out to them from the neighboring house,

“You three have business with Anatoley?”

An old man spoke across the fence. He wasn’t being unwelcoming but he genuinely wanted to know their intention.

Luka was about to say something but immediately got cut off by Lin, “Hi! I’m Lin Chen from Heilongjiang People’s Daily,” she reached into her pocket and showed him her correspondent id card, “Me and my crew here wanted to interview Comrade Ruzhin but it appeared that he isn’t home. Do you know his whereabouts?”

You have to give it to Lin, her experience as a reporter made her adaptable and spontaneous in social situation like this.

“Ah… I see. I was about to head out for grocery then I saw you guys. I didn’t know that Anatoley have this kind of fame,” he laughed, “though, I don’t know where he went. Honestly, we’re not even that close.”

“Does he have anyone we can contact? Family members or relatives, perhaps?” Lin nudged further.

He thought briefly before replied, “Well… he used to live together with his son a couple of years back. He moved out so he can live closer to work, I think. But I don’t have his contact, unfortunately.”

“It’s alright. That was already a great help. Thank you for your time!” Lin smiled professionally. The neighbor bid farewell to them before heading on his way.

“Son, huh? Let’s see…” Nadya pulled out her pocket terminal to look up someone with the same family name, and it strike one match.

Nadya let out a chuckled once she saw the name, “Dmitrey Anatoleyvich Ruzhin. It’s hereditary, I guess.”

Lin looked over to the terminal, trying to get a peek. Nadya noticed and lower it down for her to see but that when something caught her eyes.

“Wait… isn’t his place kind of close to the scrapyard?”

That discovery even made Luka who was pondering something, turned his head and come take a look.

“Yeah… That’s really is close,” Nadya concurred, “I guess we should pay him a visit.”

“Do you think he’d done it?” Lin asked.

“It’s possible but let’s not visit to him with preconceive notion. We need to hear him out first,” Nadya pushed back against rushed judgment.

After a quick final check around the perimeter, they set out for Dmitrey’s apartment. It was actually quite a distance from Anatoley’s house, almost 40 minute ride on the bus. 

The apartment Dmitrey settled in was part of the renovation campaign about a decade ago, so the build looks quite new and modern. The building was equipped with the recent tech: autonomous receptionist and cleaners. A separate elevator for delivery robot, indoor gym, game room, music room, art room, and other recreational spaces on their own separate floor. All of this is provided for free to the citizen of Pogranichny. Though it had long run out of vacancy and the extension was still under construction. With the Time Ripple, it would be delayed by a couple of years.

“I wish my company housing is this good…” Lin sarcastically exclaimed. She was still a PRC citizen, so her company provided her housing in Pogranichny but it seemed her apartment wasn’t as fancy as this one.

“My dorm is pretty old as well. It wasn’t bad but it could have more of these stuff…” Luka tried to relate. The funding of the institute likely went to the research. Since it was technically fine, the extra luxury wasn’t in the budget’s crosshair.

“Heh, my house is at least 60 years old at this point. It was my granddad’s then my mom’s then mine.” Nadya took the ownership of the house when she moved here almost 2 years ago. Her mother moved out to live with her husband in Vladivostok, so Nadya can have a place to herself. Which is probably the best considering her _Elektrifinadtsiya_ tendency.

After their grievance in front of the apartment map, they approach the receptionist, who is now a human volunteer due to the Time Ripple.

“If you’re here about the room. It’s full. And there wasn’t any further notice yet,” The receptionist said annoyingly, it seemed that these _frequently-asked questions_ should be left to the robot after all.

“No, we’re not here for that,” Nadya reached into her pocket and brought out her Millitsiya id card, “I’m Junior Lieutenant Morozova. I’m here to visit a resident named Dmitrey Ruzhin.”

Normally, an automated system would tried to confirm her identity and contact the station to verify intent but you can’t underestimate how little human care about procedure.

“Let me look it up… Dmitri—”

“With an ‘ey’,” Nadya interjected.

The receptionist rolled his eyes and mumbled, “Dmitrey… so hip and cool, huh?”

After getting a result and confirming it, the receptionist handed Nadya a keycard, “ Dmi-_Dmitrey_ is in room 704. Please return the keycard to me after you’re done.”

“Thank you, comrade” she picked up the keycard and gesture the others to the elevator.

While they are waiting for the elevator, Luka asked, “So… how are we going to approach him? Can we use Lin’s interview trick?”

“I doubt it. It works with the neighbor because he wasn’t the interviewee. Besides, he has every right to refuse a sudden interview,” Lin shutdown the idea.

“I can claim that his father was reported missing. That’s the farthest that we can go. A search needs a warrant,” Nadya reminded them of the limitation of this encounter.

“I guess that could work but would it be alright for you, Nadya? You are doing this off-duty and you also need to make up story…” Luka was concerned about Nadya reputation.

“Tarasov is going to grill me for this but we don’t have much time. First-impression is important here. We really have to sell it to Dmitrey, so that he must talk to us, no matter what,” Nadya knew that she will have to cross the line to help Luka. It was risky but it she had already set her mind to it and she will commit it all the way to the end.

The elevator door opened, a group of people walked out then the group filled in. Nadya pulled out a fresh sheet of e-paper from her cross-body bag then synced it with her pocket terminal. The missing person report with Anatoley’s mugshot and information bloomed onto the page. She quickly filled in the rest with made-up reporter and details.

The elevator eventually reached the seventh floor and they made their way through the corridor to room 704.

Before Nadya knock, she checked everything again and notice one crucial detail.

“Lin, took off your coat and give it to me,” Nadya suddenly ordered without any context.

“W-W-Why?” Lin was caught completely off-guard, her mind had already interpreted her order in _that_ way.

“Presenting yourself as a reporter would put too much pressure on him. Here, wear mine instead. I’ll hold onto yours in the meantime,” she nonchalantly replied, not realizing that she was making Lin flustered.

“A-Alright. Sure,” she took off her coat and switch it with Nadya’s instead. It was a bit bigger but it was extremely cozy and accidentally romantic.

Luka smiled and shook his head in disbelief at the sight of these two hopeless and oblivious lesbians.

“Maybe I should take off my nameplate too,” Luka commented.

“Oh yeah, that too. From now on, you two will be my _trainees_, alright?” Nadya briefed.

Once everything is ready, Nadya took a deep breath. She can’t mess this up or else their lead would end here. She raised her hand and knocked on the door…






 




